    <h2>What Is Gestational Age?</h2>

    <p>Gestational age is the age of the pregnancy counted from the first day of the last menstrual period (LMP). It is expressed in weeks and days. For example, "22 weeks 3 days" is written as 22+3.</p>

    <p>This is the standard method used by healthcare providers and recommended by ACOG. It is used to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Calculate the estimated due date</li>
      <li>Determine the trimester</li>
      <li>Schedule screening tests and ultrasounds</li>
      <li>Assess fetal growth and development</li>
      <li>Classify term (preterm, early term, full term, etc.)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why Count from the Last Period?</h2>

    <p>Pregnancy is counted from the LMP because most people know the date of their last period, but very few know the exact date of conception.</p>

    <p>This means:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>At the moment of conception, you are already considered <strong>2 weeks pregnant</strong>.</li>
      <li>At the time of a missed period, you are considered <strong>4 weeks pregnant</strong>.</li>
      <li>Your due date is <strong>40 weeks</strong> from your LMP, which is about <strong>38 weeks</strong> from conception.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Gestational Age Is Calculated</h2>

    <p><strong>Gestational age</strong> = Reference date − (LMP + cycle adjustment)</p>

    <p>The cycle adjustment accounts for cycles that are not exactly 28 days:</p>

    <p><strong>Cycle-corrected LMP</strong> = LMP + (cycle length − 28)</p>

    <p>For example, if your LMP was January 1 and your cycle is 32 days:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Cycle adjustment = 32 − 28 = 4 days</li>
      <li>Cycle-corrected LMP = January 1 + 4 days = January 5</li>
      <li>Estimated due date = January 5 + 280 days = October 12</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Gestational Age vs. Fetal Age</h2>

    <p>Gestational age and fetal age are different:</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Measurement</th>
          <th>Counted from</th>
          <th>At conception</th>
          <th>At missed period</th>
          <th>At due date</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Gestational age</td>
          <td>LMP</td>
          <td>2 weeks</td>
          <td>4 weeks</td>
          <td>40 weeks</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Fetal age</td>
          <td>Conception</td>
          <td>0 weeks</td>
          <td>2 weeks</td>
          <td>38 weeks</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Healthcare providers use gestational age. When someone says "I am 20 weeks pregnant," they mean gestational age.</p>

    <h2>How Accurate Is Gestational Age?</h2>

    <p>LMP-based dating is reasonably accurate if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your cycles are regular</li>
      <li>You know the date of your last period</li>
      <li>You are not currently breastfeeding or have recently stopped hormonal contraception</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A first-trimester ultrasound is more accurate than LMP-based dating. If the two differ by more than 7 days, ACOG recommends using the ultrasound date.</p>

    <h2>Ultrasound Dating Accuracy by Trimester</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Trimester</th>
          <th>Accuracy</th>
          <th>Method</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>First</td>
          <td>± 5 days</td>
          <td>Crown-rump length (CRL)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Second</td>
          <td>± 8-14 days</td>
          <td>Biparietal diameter, femur length</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Third</td>
          <td>± 21-28 days</td>
          <td>Too unreliable to redate</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><em>Source: ACOG Committee Opinion 700, Methods for Estimating the Due Date.</em></p>

    <h2>When to Talk to Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You are unsure of your LMP date</li>
      <li>Your cycles are irregular</li>
      <li>You have questions about your gestational age or due date</li>
      <li>Your ultrasound dating differs from your LMP dating</li>
    </ul>

